<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – 1914 CE · capital Zungeru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate built on the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate, where Frederick Lugard developed the system of indirect rule through the emirs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 January 1900 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took over the northern lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-nigeria-protectorate">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> as high commissioner. Its capital was first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> and from 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zungeru">Zungeru</a>. Between 1900 and 1903 British-led troops conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> in 1903.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With few officials and little money, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Lugard</a> governed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> who accepted British authority, keeping their courts, taxes and officials under the supervision of British residents. This <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, set out in his Political Memoranda, became a model for British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Christian missions were restricted in Muslim areas, and Western schooling spread slowly. A revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satiru-uprising">Satiru</a> in 1906 was crushed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Lugard</a> returned as governor of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> in 1912 and merged them on 1 January 1914 into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>. The north remained a separate administrative region, and the legacy of separate development shaped Nigerian politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria.jpg" alt="Protectorate of Northern Nigeria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Northern_Nigeria_Protectorate_(1900%E2%80%931914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zungeru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick Lugard, Percy Girouard</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hausa, Fulfulde, Kanuri, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Northern Nigeria, Northern Nigeria Protectorate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1907&amp;focus=protectorate-of-northern-nigeria" aria-label="Protectorate of Northern Nigeria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 1812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Protectorate of Northern Nigeria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5170 2068L5144 1947M5170 2068L5187 2202M5170 2068L4997 651M5170 2068L5094 2192M5170 2068L5234 1986M5170 2068L5209 1950M5170 2068L5212 2021M5170 2068L5198 2091M5170 2068L5124 1959M5170 2068L5131 2028M5170 2068L5128 1967M5170 2068L5345 2090M5170 2068L5271 2049M5170 2068L5308 2050M5170 2068L5276 1970M5170 2068L5151 2046M5170 2068L5166 2094M5170 2068L5271 2140M5170 2068L5362 1992"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5144 1947h0M5187 2202h0M4997 651h0M5094 2192h0M5234 1986h0M5209 1950h0M5212 2021h0M5198 2091h0M5124 1959h0M5131 2028h0M5128 1967h0M5345 2090h0M5271 2049h0M5308 20